REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF KOTABARU REGENCY

and

REGENT OF KOTABARU

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

1. The implementation of protection of women and children is carried out based on the principles of:
a. respect for the rights of women and children;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination in the empowerment of women and children;
d. the best interests of women and children; and
e. restoration of social and economic rights for women and children who experience acts of violence or neglect.
2. The objectives of implementing protection of women and children include:
a. preventing acts of violence or neglect against women and children including trafficking in persons;
b. eliminating all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;c. protecting and providing a sense of security for women and children;
d. providing services to women and children victims of acts of violence, reporters, and witnesses;
e. facilitating and mediating household disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household; and
f. seeking compensation/compensation for losses suffered by victims from perpetrators of violence.

CHAPTER IV

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

Article 4

1. Every woman and child who becomes a victim of physical violence, exploitation or human trafficking has the right to receive protection and services.
2. Protection and services for victims as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. complaints or requests for protection from the person concerned;
b. confidential handling or placement of victims in safe locations and houses whose whereabouts are kept secret to avoid intimidation and threats;
c. medical services/health treatment in the form of care and recovery of injuries or physical condition of victims by medical personnel and paramedics;
d. medicolegal services for legal evidence;
e. protection by law enforcement officers;
f. provision of information regarding legal rights and obligations to victims or guardians of victims and identification of incidents;
g. continuous handling up to the Rehabilitation stage;
h. psychological assistance to restore the traumatic condition of victims and restore the self-confidence of victims;
i. assistance support for legal processes and obtaining compensation or compensation rights;
j. guarantee of rights related to status as family members and community members (social reintegration); and
k. economic independence services in the form of skills training and providing economic access so that victims can be independent.
3. In the event that the victim is a child, in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in paragraph (2), also receives special rights including:
a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma;
e. the right to obtain freedom; and
f. the right to obtain compensation from the perpetrator.

Article 6

The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 5 letter a, include:
a. implementing the obligations of protection of women and children from acts of violence established by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs and activities for the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. providing shelters for women and children victims of violence;
f. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence according to regional financial capacity; and
g. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence.

Article 11

1. The organizational structure of P2TP2A consists of:
a. Protector/Supervisor;
b. Advisor;
c. Coordinator;
d. Chairperson;
e. Vice Chairpersonf. Secretary;
g. Vice Secretary;
h. Treasurer; and
i. Members.
2. Coordinator consists of:
a. Assistance and Advocacy Division;
b. Service and Recovery Division;
c. Network and Information Strengthening Division;
d. Education and Training Division;
e. Case Manager; and
f. Expert Team.
3. Further provisions regarding the organizational structure of P2TP2A and description of coordinator duties are further regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 12

P2TP2A builds functional networks with:
a. Police;
b. Prosecutor's Office;
c. Court;
d. Hospital/Community Health Center;
e. Psychiatrist; and
f. Other institutions that have common goals.

Article 17

1. The form of prevention as referred to in Article 16 is carried out through:
a. socialization activities of laws and regulations related to the protection of Children and Women to the wider community in the region;
b. dissemination activities of the existence of P2TP2A to be known by everyone in the region through the distribution of brochures, leaflets, pamphlets or notice boards so that incidents of violence, exploitation and neglect can be reported as soon as possible and can be handled before reaching the worst level;
c. training for members who are able to disseminate norms of prohibitions and sanctions to the community;
d. opening of public complaint channels by the Regional Government or P2TP2A in each area through community networks;
e. strengthening coordination channels with Sub-district, Village/Sub-district Government;
f. immediate handling of public complaints in all areas of the region and securing subjects of acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking in persons or neglect; and/or
g. counseling/socialization in the form of raising awareness of the wider community down to the smallest community groups (families), especially parents to protect women and children.
2. The Regional Government through P2TP2A conducts data collection on areas of violence based on the incidence rate and conducts research and assessment of the vulnerability level of areas to find solutions for reducing to stopping acts of violence, exploitation and neglect in vulnerable areas.

Article 20

The implementation of services and assistance to victims is carried out with the principles of:
a. non-discrimination;
b. fast, safe, and empathetic;
c. coordination between government agencies;
d. guarantee of confidentiality;
e. easily accessible; and
f. free of charge.

Article 21

1. The Regional Government through P2TP2A is obliged to carry out economic empowerment of victims of acts of violence, exploitation and/or trafficking or neglect.
2. The form of empowerment of victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:
a. providing the necessary needs for entrepreneurship training for victims of acts of violence, in order to increase knowledge, attitudes, and business skills;
b. facilitating the implementation of various job training and skills training;
c. providing assistance in developing productive economic businesses;
d. exploring cooperation with small, medium and large companies, as well as financial institutions to develop businesses of victims of acts of violence;
e. seeking the provision of capital for victims of acts of violence; and
f. expanding access to information and promoting the products of victims of acts of violence.

Article 22

1. Cooperation is carried out to strengthen the role of P2TP2A in achieving the objectives of protecting women and children in the regional area.
2. Cooperation is carried out with:
a. the government;
b. the provincial government;
c. other regency/city governments;
d. non-governmental institutions; and
e. international institutions whose existence is recognized.
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2), includes:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of acts of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration; and
d. provision of evidence and witnesses, and followed up based on the provisions of laws and regulations.
4. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), is set forth in the form of a joint agreement.

CHAPTER IX

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 24

1. In implementing protection for women and children, the community can:
a. form family partners at the village/sub-district level by the community;
b. form women and children protection units within community organizations;
c. conduct socialization of women's and children's rights independently;
d. provide first aid to victims; and
e. report to the competent authorities if violence against victims occurs in their environment.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and mass media.

CHAPTER XII

INVESTIGATION

Article 28

1. In addition to general investigation officials, investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children in this Regional Regulation may also be conducted by Civil Servant Investigators whose appointment is in accordance with laws and regulations.
2. Civil Servant Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1), are authorized to:
a. receive reports or complaints from a person regarding the existence of criminal acts of violence against women and children;
b. take first action at that time at the scene and conduct examination;
c. interrogate a suspect and examine the suspect's identification;
d. seize objects and/or documents;
e. take fingerprints and photograph a person;
f. summon persons to be heard and examined as suspects or witnesses;
g. bring in expert witnesses required in connection with the examination of the case;
h. terminate the investigation after receiving instructions from the General Investigator that there is insufficient evidence, or the incident does not constitute a criminal act and subsequently through the General Investigator notify this matter to the public prosecutor, the suspect or their family; and/or
i. take other actions necessary for the smooth investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children, which according to law can be accounted for.
3. The Investigator as referred to in paragraph (2), shall notify the commencement of the investigation and submit the results of the investigation to the public prosecutor through the Investigator Official of the Police of the Republic of Indonesia, in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the Criminal Procedure Law.

Article 32

In the case that the perpetrator of a criminal act is a child, the following matters apply:
a. the legal process is carried out humanely by viewing the child as still having hope for their future;
b. The Investigator must be a person who has competence in special investigation for child handling cases;
c. Investigation is conducted in a closed room and attended only by the advocacy team and the child's parents/guardian;
d. in the investigation process, the child is not placed in general detention, but is entrusted to their family with supervision and custody;
e. the trial process is carried out specifically in the juvenile court trial room; and
f. the child's sentencing process is placed in a special juvenile correctional institution, with a transition period that may be accompanied by their parents.
